Hi [~accountid:6052891960d39e006f72f0f0] I apologize for the delayed reply. 

Regarding Croatia, please find below answers to your questions: 

# Should we set up a new local company for that or not? I think here we need to discuss this also with tax and transfer pricing(?) as usually it all comes to taxation. Tax should also prepare intra-group agreements in that case.
## {color:#ff5630}Legally, it's unnecessary to establish a new entity; the existing one can engage in Bolt Food business operations, as can localized rentals (the same is with rentals that are already localized in HR). Our main competitor in HR, WOLT,  is already using the localized model. {color}
## {color:#ff5630}We would only need to register additional business activities. The costs would be approx. EUR 500{color}
# If we would set up a new local company, what is the cost for Bolt to set up a local company and to maintain the company? Also intra-group agreements are relevant here.
## {color:#ff5630}Costs related to opening a local entity would be approx EUR 1,000 - 1,500{color}
# Reclassification risk - I just started wondering whether or not using Estonian company makes it harder for couriers to claim employer-employee relationship? Maybe couriers are more willing to go to the court against a local company?
## {color:#ff5630}Correct, psychologically it is easier for couriers to initiate legal proceedings against the local entity, particularly when it comes to enforcing any potential judgments, however, legally the process of initiating claims against foreign legal entities (especially with the seat in the EU) is not that complex. {color}

