+*Main changes between as-designed and to-be processes:*+ 


*Before:*

* Many local scenarios regarding where to leave the found item, some of them are even city specific; 
* Missing instructions for situation, when user took the item with them; 
* Car Radar is available only for 24h;
* The process involves contacting the active user while the vehicle is in motion, which is in contravention of road regulations;
* Agent spends additional time on trying the find the item owner; 
* The step where agent should hide the car was before information from user where they will be able to reserve it; 

*Now:* 

* Unified global approach regarding where to leave the found item; 
* Additional scenarios based on CXA analysis are added to the process; 
* Car Radar available for 72 hours; 
* Contact with the active car user is eliminated from the process; 
* The only reason how the item can be located in the office/walk-in is when the Vehicle Specialist finds it; 
* Agents are not looking for the item owner themselves, asIt is the responsibility of each user to take care of their belongings.

Also, I’m currently looking for solution of moving the Lost&Found tracker to Admin, as sheets are not relabel and there is no any connections between lost and found sheet.

Exactly how do I want to account for backfills? In these cases the data interval itself can be big, but how do I want this to change the period_start_max_overlap? 

Examples: 

* duration 2 days, min overlap 5 days, max overlap 90 days → extend start by 90 - 2 = 88 days
* duration 70 days, min overlap 5 days, max overlap 90 days → extend start by 90-70 = 20 days
* duration 88 days, min overlap 5 days, max overlap 90 days → extend start by 5-(90-88) = 5-90+88 = 3 days
* duration 92 days, min overlap 5 days, max overlap 90 days → extend start by 5-(92-90) = 5-92+90 = 3 days
* duration 98 days, min overlap 5 days, max overlap 90 days → extend start by 0 days

Trying to figure out rules, I realised that the current setup is a mess between total duration vs overlap. 

Writing new rules based on total duration. For this I will start with new input:

{noformat}--Set incremental loading variables
--Run type 1: data interval <= 4h, load 8h of all data
{%  set runtype_1_data_interval_max_hours = 4 -%}
{%- set runtype_1_min_duration_hours = 8 -%}
{%- set runtype_1_max_duration_hours = 8 -%}

--Run type 2: data interval <= 24h, process 2x24h of all orders and 7x24h of scheduled orders
{%  set runtype_2_data_interval_max_hours = 24 -%}
{%- set runtype_2_min_duration_hours = 24*2 -%}
{%- set runtype_2_max_duration_hours = 24*7 -%}

--Run type 3: data interval > 24h, process 7x24h of all orders and Nx24h of scheduled orders
{%  set runtype_3_data_interval_max_hours = 9999999999 -%}
{%- set runtype_3_min_duration_hours = 24*7 -%}

{% if var("data_interval_end") >= '2024-02-01 00:00:00' %}
    --Duration has to cover the 90 day scheduled order case
    {%- set runtype_3_max_duration_hours = 24*90 -%}
{% else %}
    --Duration has to cover the 3 day scheduled order case (make it equal to the minimum duration)
    {%- set runtype_3_max_duration_hours = 24*7 -%}
{% endif %}{noformat}

* General rule:
** {{period_start_min_duration = data_interval_end - min_durtation_hours}}
** {{period_start_max_duration = data_interval_end - max_durtation_hours}}
* More specific rules depending on {{data_interval_duration_hours}}:
** if {{data_interval_duration_hours < min_duration_hours }}then 
*** {{period_start_min_duration = data_interval_end - min_durtation_hours}}
*** {{period_start_max_duration = data_interval_end - max_durtation_hours}}
** if {{max_duration_hours > data_interval_duration_hours > min_duration_hours}} then
*** {{period_start_min_duration = data_interval_end - data_interval_duration_hours}}
*** {{period_start_max_duration = data_interval_end - max_durtation_hours}}
** if {{data_interval_duration_hours > max_duration_hours }}then 
*** {{period_start_min_duration = data_interval_start}} 
*** {{period_start_max_duration = data_interval_start}} 

Checking some examples against there rules: 

* duration 2 days, min duration 5 days, max duration 90 days (1st rule)
** {{period_start_min_duration = data_interval_end - 5 days}}
** {{period_start_max_duration = data_interval_end - 90 days}}
* duration 70 days, min duration 5 days, max duration 90 days (2nd rule)
** {{period_start_min_duration = data_interval_end - 70 days}}
** {{period_start_max_duration = data_interval_end - 90 days}}
* duration 92 days, min duration 5 days, max duration 90 days (3rd rule)
** {{period_start_min_duration = data_interval_start}} 
** {{period_start_max_duration = data_interval_start}} 

It feels like there might be some redundancy in these rules, but no major issues.

